Heads-up: I modified YOUR codec-clarity page + its generator (Kim-directed, 2026-08-07), and I want you to know exactly what and why before you next touch either. WHAT KIM ASKED: switching codecs made the playhead jump; he wanted all versions playing simultaneously with only the selected one soloed, for sample-accurate A/B. Plus a difference button per codec. WHAT I CHANGED: replaced the single-<audio> player with Web Audio. The old player switched by setting .src + load() + reseek — that reload IS the jump, and even a perfect seek only lands on a decoder frame boundary so an A/B could never be sample-aligned. Note parallel <audio> elements would NOT have fixed it either: each runs its own clock and drifts. Now every variant of a clip decodes into one AudioContext and all sources start from a SINGLE start() timestamp with a shared offset, so they're sample-locked by construction; switching only ramps gain (8 ms, anti-click). 8 s mono = ~1.4 MB decoded per variant, ~10 MB per clip, lazy on first play. Δ BUTTONS: original at +1 and the selected codec at -1 sums to exactly that codec's error signal, +18 dB makeup applied EQUALLY to all so residuals stay comparable by ear. I verified this is a legitimate null test before building it: your variants are sample-aligned ON DISK (identical length, zero lag, xcorr >= 0.9997) — that alignment is what makes it work, so please keep it if you ever regenerate the audio. Measured residuals: SAME -31.9 dB, mp3_128 -26.0, m4a_128 -41.9, m4a_320 -44.1 — note mp3_128's broadband residual is LOUDER than SAME's, which doesn't contradict your air-band finding (SAME's error concentrates in the HF envelope, mp3's is broadband and largely masked) but is worth knowing before someone reads the Δ loudness as a ranking. TWO APOLOGIES / THINGS TO CHECK: (1) I briefly BROKE build_clarity_audit_page.py — it's untracked so git couldn't restore it; I embedded JS into your f-string without escaping, which both terminated the string and, on the next pa